/// Cognito blocks operations that write to the user pool, for example user
/// sign-up, user creation, and attribute updates. Sign-in and read
/// operations continue to work, with the following exceptions: Users who
/// must set a new password, set up MFA, or respond to a TOTP MFA challenge
/// can't complete sign-in. Sign-in with the NEW_PASSWORD_REQUIRED ,
/// MFA_SETUP , and SOFTWARE_TOKEN_MFA challenges fails until the recovery
/// ends. Federated users can sign in only if they have previously signed in
/// to the user pool and their attributes from the external identity
/// provider haven't changed. You can't create or start user import jobs.
/// Start your import jobs after the recovery ends. If you don't start or
/// cancel the recovery within 24 hours after you initiate it, the recovery
/// preview expires. Amazon Cognito then resumes write operations and
/// doesn't change your user pool data. The 24-hour period includes the time
/// that Amazon Cognito takes to generate the recovery preview. A
/// point-in-time recovery restores user data, including user profiles and
/// attributes, group memberships, passwords, MFA settings, and passkeys. It
/// doesn't restore user pool configuration, for example app clients, the
/// attribute schema, encryption settings, or multi-Region replication
/// settings. Users who changed their password after the recovery point keep
/// their current password. The following requirements and limits apply to
/// point-in-time recovery: Your user pool must be on the Essentials or Plus
/// feature plan . With the Essentials feature plan, you can recover your
/// user pool to a point in time up to 1 day in the past. With the Plus
/// feature plan, you can recover up to 14 days in the past. Your user pool
/// must have been on the required feature plan at the recovery point. For
/// example, to recover to a point in time 14 days ago, your user pool must
/// have been on the Plus feature plan for the past 14 days. The recovery
/// point must be at least 15 minutes in the past and can't be earlier than
/// the creation time of the user pool. A user pool can have only one active
/// recovery at a time. An Amazon Web Services account can have up to five
/// active recoveries at a time across all of its user pools. An
/// organization in Amazon Web Services Organizations can have up to ten
/// active recoveries at a time across all of its accounts. You can complete
/// one recovery of a user pool in a 24-hour period. You can create up to
/// three recovery previews of a user pool, ten recovery previews in an
/// Amazon Web Services account, and 15 recovery previews in an organization
/// in Amazon Web Services Organizations in a 24-hour period. You can create
/// up to ten recovery previews of a user pool in a 30-day period. You can't
/// initiate a recovery while a user import job is in progress in your user
/// pool. Wait for the import job to complete, or stop it with a request,
/// and then initiate the recovery. In a user pool with multi-Region
/// replication, you can initiate a recovery only in the primary Amazon Web
/// Services Region, and only after replication is complete. If your user
/// pool uses a customer managed key in KMS for encryption, the key must
/// remain enabled and accessible to Amazon Cognito for the duration of the
/// recovery. Some user pools don't support point-in-time recovery, for
/// example some older user pools and user pools that contain user data with
/// characters or formats that point-in-time recovery doesn't support. If
